   346	
   347	## 5. Removing the tangency hypothesis (Theorem C, nodalization)
   348	
   349	Prop. 3.27 genuinely needs `D` nodal: in its proof the normalisation `H` must "intersect
   350	the closure of `q^{-1}(R) - H` transversally" in order to invoke Nori's Lemma 1.4, and at
   351	an `A_{2k-1}` the other branch meets `H` with multiplicity `k`. Two independent repairs
   352	follow; the second is complete.
   353	
   354	**5.1 Fallback (literature-only): blow up the tangential points.** Following Nori's own
   355	recipe (p. 307), blow up each `A_{2k_p-1}` point `k_p` times — each blow-up has
   356	multiplicity `2`, and after `k_p` of them the two branches are separated and meet the
   357	last exceptional curve transversally at distinct points, so the strict transform is
   358	smooth there. Put the exceptional curves into `E`; then `X'' - E = C^2 - {tangential
   359	points}` is still simply connected and `X'' - (D u E) = C^2 - D` is unchanged. The cost
   360	is `sum_j m_j^2 = 4 k_p` per tangential point, and `2r` now counts only the `s_1`
   361	remaining nodes. With `K_tac := sum_{p : k_p >= 2} k_p` and `delta_aff = s_1 + K_tac`,
   362	Nori's inequality `C'^2 - 4 K_tac > 2 s_1` becomes, on the coprime stratum,
   363	`nd - 4K_tac > (n-1)(d-1) - 2K_tac`, i.e.
   364	
   365	```text
   366	(B')     2 K_tac <= n + d - 2      ==>   pi_1(C^2 - D) abelian   ==>   NO.
   367	```
   368	
   369	`K_tac = 0` recovers Theorem B. `(B')` is unconditional and uses only the hashed Nori.
   370	
   371	**5.2 The complete repair: nodalization inside the parameter space.** Fix `gcd(n,d)=1`
   372	and let `P_{d,n} = {(p,q) : deg p = d, deg q = n}`, an irreducible quasi-affine variety
   373	(a Zariski-open subset of `C^{d+1} x C^{n+1}`). Every member is birational onto its image
   374	(a factorisation `gamma = gamma' o psi` with `deg psi = e` would force `e | gcd(d,n)=1`)
   375	and has one place at infinity at `Q = [1:0:0]`.
   376	
   377	**LEMMA 5.3 (`delta` is constant).** *On `P_{d,n}` with `gcd(n,d)=1`,
   378	`delta_infty = (d-n-1)(d-1)/2` and hence `delta_aff = (n-1)(d-1)/2` are constant.*
   379	*Proof.* The germ at `Q` is always parametrized by `(v,u) = (s^{d-n} beta, s^d alpha)`
   380	with `gcd(d-n,d)=1`, i.e. is always the `(d-n, d)`-cusp; then `(G)`. `[]`
   381	
   382	**LEMMA 5.4 (generic member is nodal).** *If `n >= 3` and `d >= 4`, the generic member of
   383	`P_{d,n}` is an immersion with only ordinary double points and no triple point.*
   384	*Proof.* Coefficient count: `{1,t,...,t^d}` and `{1,...,t^n}` separate `1`-jets at any
   385	two distinct parameters once `d, n >= 3`, so on `P_{d,n}` the conditions
   386	(i) `gamma'(t) = 0` (2 conditions on a 1-parameter family of `t`),
   387	(ii) `gamma(t_1) = gamma(t_2)` **and** the tangents agree (3 conditions on a 2-parameter
   388	family of pairs),
   389	(iii) `gamma(t_1) = gamma(t_2) = gamma(t_3)` (4 conditions on a 3-parameter family)
   390	are each independent, so each bad locus has codimension `>= 1` in `P_{d,n}`. `[]`
   391	
   392	(The hypothesis `n >= 3` is exactly what Theorem A(3) supplies once `phi` is assumed;
   393	`n <= 2` is already dead by Theorem A.)
   394	
   395	**LEMMA 5.5 (local shape of the degeneration).** *Let `D_0 in P_{d,n}` and let `D_t -> D_0`
   396	be nodal members. By 5.3 the total double-point length `delta_aff` is constant, so at
   397	each `p in Sing D_0` of type `A_{2k_p-1}` the length-`k_p` piece of the double-point
   398	scheme of `D_0` (supported at the single pair `(t_1^0, t_2^0)`) spreads into exactly
   399	`k_p` reduced pairs near `(t_1^0,t_2^0)`. Hence near `p`, `D_t` is two smooth branches —
   400	deformations of the two branches of `D_0` — crossing transversally at `k_p` points.*
   401	
   402	**LEMMA 5.6 (braid monodromy comparison).** *With the vertical projection of §1 fixed for
   403	both curves, and `t` small, choose disjoint discs `Y_i` in the `x`-line, one around each
   404	critical value of `D_0`, containing all critical values of `D_t` that converge to it.
   405	Then:*
   406	1. *over `partial Y_i` the two curves are isotopic, so the braid of `partial Y_i` is the
   407	   same factor `beta_i` for `D_0` and for `D_t`;*
   408	2. *for a disc around a vertical tangency or a node of `D_0` nothing splits, and the
   409	   relation is unchanged;*
   410	3. *for a disc `Y` around an `A_{2k-1}` point, the two relevant strands stay inside a
   411	   thin tube while the other `d-2` strands stay away, so all local braids lie in a single
   412	   `B_2 = <sigma>` conjugated into `B_d` by one fixed braid `w`. `B_2` is abelian, so the
   413	   `k` factors of `D_t` inside `Y` are all equal to `w sigma^2 w^{-1}`, and the `k`
   414	   relations they impose are the single relation `[w(xi_a), w(xi_b)] = 1`, while `D_0`
   415	   imposes `[ (w(xi_a) w(xi_b))^k , w(xi_a) ] = 1`.*
   416	*Consequently*
   417	
   418	```text
   419	(*)   pi_1(C^2 - D_t) = pi_1(C^2 - D_0) / << [w_p(xi_a), w_p(xi_b)] : p tangential >> .
   420	```
   421	
   422	**THEOREM C.** *Let `D` be as in §1 with `gcd(n,d) = 1`, tangency allowed. Then
   423	`pi_1(C^2-D)` has no surjection onto `S_4` sending meridians to transpositions and the
   424	two local meridians at each double point to disjoint transpositions.*
   425	
   426	*Proof.* Suppose `phi` exists. By Theorem A(3), `n >= 3` and `d >= 4`. By 5.4 pick a
   427	nodal `D_t in P_{d,n}` close to `D`; by 5.3 and 5.5 the degeneration is locally as in
   428	5.5, and by 5.6 the group `pi_1(C^2-D_t)` is the quotient `(*)`. By §2, `phi` sends the
   429	two local meridians at each tangential point to **disjoint** transpositions, which
   430	commute; so `phi` kills every relator on the right-hand side of `(*)` and therefore
   431	factors through `pi_1(C^2 - D_t)`. `D_t` is nodal with `gcd(n,d) = 1`, so Theorem B makes
   432	`pi_1(C^2-D_t)` abelian. Hence `im(phi)` is abelian, contradicting `im(phi) = S_4`. `[]`
   433	
   434	Two remarks. First, the deformation is used only to *transport a group-theoretic
   435	consequence*, never to claim `D` itself is nodal; the direction of Zariski's
   436	specialisation principle (special complement surjects onto general complement) is the
   437	direction actually used. Second, Nori's own invariant for arbitrary singularities gives
   438	independent corroboration: by Def. 3.25, `sum_P A(C';P) = delta_aff` for our curves, so
   439	`B(C') = C'^2 - 2 delta_aff = n + d - 1 > 0` **identically on the coprime stratum**
   440	(Lemma 4.4). Any extension of Prop. 3.27 from "nodal" to "`B(C) > 0`" — the shape Nori's
   441	3.26 already has — would give Theorem C directly, without the deformation. Nori does not
   442	state that extension, so it is not consumed; `(*)` is what carries the proof.
   443	
   444	<!-- SECTION 5 END -->
   445	
   446	## 6. Main theorem and decision
   447	
   448	**MAIN THEOREM.** *Let `D subset C^2` be an irreducible polynomial curve with
   449	`normalization(D) = A^1`, one place at infinity `Q`, and every affine singularity a double
   450	point of two smooth branches (tangency allowed). Assume the intrinsic coprimality
   451	condition*
   452	
   453	```text
   454	(C1)     gcd( mult_Q(Dbar) , I(Dbar, L_infty; Q) ) = 1
   455	```
   456	
   457	*(equivalently `gcd(deg p, deg q) = 1` after the normalization of §1; equivalently the
   458	place at infinity has exactly one Puiseux pair). Then `pi_1(C^2 - D)` admits **no**
   459	surjection onto `S_4` carrying every meridian to a transposition and the two local
   460	meridians at each double point to disjoint transpositions.*
   461	
   462	*Proof.* Theorem C, which invokes Theorem A for `n >= 3`, Lemmas 5.3–5.6, and
   463	Theorem B. `[]`
   464	
   465	**Scope of the stronger, abelian conclusion.** `pi_1(C^2-D) = Z` is proved here on `(C1)`
   466	for `D` nodal (Theorem B) and for `D` tangential with `2 K_tac <= n + d - 2` (`(B')`).
   467	For the remaining tangential curves on `(C1)`, what is proved is the
   468	representation-level statement of the Main Theorem — which is exactly what PI1-S4 asks
   469	— and not abelianness; Theorem C routes `phi` through the abelian group
   470	`pi_1(C^2 - D_t)` of a nearby nodal curve without asserting that the two groups agree.
   471	This distinction is recorded rather than smoothed over: an unqualified "the complement is
   472	abelian in the whole class" would be an overclaim.
   473	
   474	**Decision: NO on the coprime stratum, unconditionally.** No YES candidate exists there —
   475	not merely "none found": Theorems B/C forbid one. Off `(C1)` the answer is typed
   476	`OPEN[PI1S4-NONCOPRIME]` (§9), with the missing input a purely **local, numerical**
   477	statement about the place at infinity — no group theory and no new topology.
   478	
   479	**What this does and does not do for the campaign.** Integration §2 asks PI1-S4 for the
   480	curve `D_1` of the repaired residual (Thm 4.3(1)–(4) + gate). The Main Theorem closes

