   552	## 9. Residual: typed OPEN and successor
   553	
   554	**`OPEN[PI1S4-NONCOPRIME]`.** *PI1-S4 for curves violating `(C1)`, i.e. with
   555	`g := gcd(mult_Q(Dbar), I(Dbar,L_infty;Q)) = gcd(deg p, deg q) >= 2`, equivalently with
   556	two or more Puiseux pairs at infinity.* Exactly two inputs are missing, both narrow:
   557	
   558	**(i) Local, numerical — the only thing Theorem B needs.** For the place at infinity of a
   559	polynomial curve with `mult_Q(Dbar) = d-n` and `I(Dbar,L_infty;Q) = d`, prove
   560	
   561	```text
   562	(M-INF)   M_infty := sum_j m_j  <=  3d - 3
   563	```
   564	
   565	over the minimal blow-up sequence making the strict transform smooth and transverse to
   566	`sigma^{-1}(L_infty)` at a smooth point of it. By Lemma 4.3 this is *equivalent* to
   567	`C'^2 > 2 delta_aff`, hence gives Nori's inequality for every `s <= delta_aff`, hence
   568	gives Theorem B verbatim in the non-coprime case. Status: **proved** when `g = 1`
   569	(Lemma 4.4, `M_infty = 2d-n-1 <= 2d-2`); **verified** in the smallest non-coprime worked
   570	case `(d,n) = (4,2)`, `gamma(t) = (t^4, t^2+t)`, whose place at infinity is a `(2,5)`-cusp
   571	with multiplicity sequence `2,2,1,1`, so `M_infty = 6 <= 9 = 3d-3` (and `C'^2 = 6 > 2 = 2s`);
   572	**not proved** in general. Note the crude route fails: `M_infty <= 2 delta_infty + tail`
   573	is far too weak, and `M_infty <= 2b-1` is false for germs of small `I(B,L)`, so the proof
   574	must use `I(Dbar,L_infty;Q) = d` (the shared-cluster identity
   575	`sum_{j in S} m_j = d`, `S` an initial segment) together with `mult_Q = d-n`. The
   576	residual content is a bound on `sum_{j notin S} m_j` by `2d-3`.
   577	
   578	**(ii) Global, one line of geometry — what Theorem C needs.** Either
   579	(ii-a) an **equisingular-at-infinity nodalization**: a locally closed subvariety of
   580	`P_{d,n}` containing `D` on which `delta_infty` is constant and whose generic member is
   581	nodal (when `g >= 2`, `delta_infty` is *not* constant on all of `P_{d,n}`, so Lemma 5.3
   582	fails and Lemma 5.5's length bookkeeping breaks); or
   583	(ii-b) an extension of Nori's Prop. 3.27 replacing the hypothesis "`D` is nodal,
   584	`C^2 > 2r(C)`" by "`B(C) > 0`" in the sense of his own Def. 3.25. (ii-b) is strictly
   585	better: it would delete §5 entirely, in the coprime case too, since `B(C') = n+d-1 > 0`
   586	there identically. Nori proves the `B(C)`-form only in 3.26, under `C cap R = empty`,
   587	which does not apply; the obstruction in the 3.27 proof is precisely that the
   588	normalisation `H` must meet the rest of `q^{-1}(D u E)` transversally, which fails at an
   589	`A_{2k-1}` for `k >= 2`.
   590	
   591	**Without (i) and (ii), the following are already unconditional off `(C1)`:**
   592	`p(rho_infty)` is a `d`-cycle; `V = d-1`; `chi(Y) = 3`; §2's local verdict; and, whenever
   593	`(M-INF)` is checked for the particular curve, the criterion
   594	`C'^2 > 2 s_1 + 4 K_tac` (the `(B')` bookkeeping) decides that curve.
   595	
   596	**Successor's first move (route 1, non-coprime).** For `g >= 2` the braid at infinity is
   597	an iterated-torus (cable) braid, not `delta^n`, so Theorem A's proof does not transcribe.
   598	The natural attempt is to collapse cables: group the `d` strands into `p_1` blocks of size
   599	`d/p_1`, take block products `Pi_1,...,Pi_{p_1}` in `S_4`, and check whether the induced
   600	action of `rho_infty` on `(Pi_1,...,Pi_{p_1})` is the Hurwitz action of
   601	`delta_{p_1}^{q_1}` (the internal full twists conjugate each block by its own product and
   602	so act trivially on that product). If so, the Theorem A argument reruns at the outer
   603	level and yields `(Pi_1 ... Pi_{p_1})^{q_1} = Pi^{q_1} = 1` provided
   604	`<Pi_1,...,Pi_{p_1}>` is centreless. **This is stated as the successor's hypothesis, not
   605	as a result**: neither the block-collapse compatibility nor the centrelessness is
   606	established here, and no gap above is filled by it.
   607	
