59:**Affine recast, sourced from Neumann–Norbury (arXiv math/0110286, Thm. 2.1), quoting Nori 3.27.** Let `Σ ⊂ C²` be nodal. Let `X` be a blow-up of `P²` resolving the singularities of `Σ` *at infinity*, with `D = X − C²`. If the proper transform of each component satisfies `C̄_i · C̄_i > 2 r(Σ_i)` and meets `D` transversally, then `π₁(C² − Σ)` is abelian (because `X − D = C²` is simply connected, so `N` is the whole group). This is Nori 3.27 with `E` the divisor at infinity, not a new theorem.
63:## 2. Caveat (a): projective nodal theorems vs affine `D` with one place at infinity
68:- One place at infinity (normalisation `A¹`) means `D̄ ∩ L_∞ = {Q}` as a set, with `I(D̄, L_∞; Q) = deg D̄ = d`. For `d ≥ 2` this is not a node. Two places (`C^*`) give either two points or one point with two branches; transverse meeting with `L_∞` occurs only if those intersection multiplicities are 1, i.e. `d` points, which is the generic-line case, not one or two places.
74:Nori 3.27 with `X = P²`, `D = D̄`, `E = L_∞` would give exactly `π₁(C² − D)` abelian, *if* `D̄` were nodal, `D̄` met `L_∞` transversally, and `C² > 2 r(C)`. The numerical bound holds for irreducible plane curves; the first two hypotheses fail at infinity.
76:The Neumann–Norbury recast (blow up until `D̄` meets the exceptional divisor of infinity transversally, then apply 3.27 on that surface) repairs transversality, but still requires the affine curve to be *nodal* and the proper transform to satisfy the numerical bound. It does not cover tacnodes, and it does not apply until the infinite germ has been resolved to a nodal (or smooth) meeting.
78:Zariski’s flex theorem (Oka ICTP (I) and statement (3); Cogolludo, *Braid monodromy of algebraic curves*, Thm. 2.13, hashed Numdam AMBP 2011): if an irreducible curve of degree `d` has a line `L` with `L ∩ C = {Q}` and `Q` a *smooth* point (flex of order `d−2`, i.e. `I(C,L;Q) = d`), then `π₁(C²_L − C)` is abelian — the local monodromy is `G(d,1) ≅ Z`, all meridians of a generic fibre become equal, so the group is cyclic. Cogolludo’s projective Thm. 2.13 drops smoothness of `Q` but concludes only `π₁(P² − C)` abelian, which is the wrong group (see Oka Remark 3). Oka Prop. 23: a *single* unibranch singularity with a maximal tangent also makes both groups abelian; our `D` may have additional affine double points, so Prop. 23 does not apply.
80:**Partial sourced YES, `A¹` with smooth infinite place.** A polynomial immersion `γ: C → C²`, `γ(t) = (p(t), q(t))`, is birational onto its image with one place at infinity. If `|deg p − deg q| = 1`, the unique infinite point of `D̄` is a smooth flex of order `d−2` (differential of the compactified parametrisation is non-zero at `∞`; this derivative computation is an inference from the local chart `s = 1/t`). Zariski–Oka (3) then gives `π₁(C² − D) ≅ Z`. This includes every nodal cubic with the flex at infinity, and every polynomial curve of consecutive degrees. It is *not* the boxed lemma: the boxed class also allows `|deg p − deg q| ≥ 2` (unibranch singular infinity) and normalisation `C^*`.
97:| Oka smooth affine curve with `π₁ ≅ B₃` (Neumann–Norbury p. 4, equation `(x²−y)² y − 4x(x²−y)+4 = 0`) | `B₃` | not verified here to have normalisation `A¹` or `C^*` (degree 5, two points at infinity; genus not computed; fail-closed: not used as a countermodel) |
101:Orevkov, Math. USSR Sb. 65 (1990) (statement recovered from the author’s posted text at `math.univ-toulouse.fr/~orevkov/fg.pdf`; the PDF itself 504’d, so this sentence is from the HTML abstract and introduction, *not* a hashed PDF): if `K ⊂ C²` has only nodes *and* satisfies the “negativity condition at infinity”, then `π₁(C² − K)` is generated by one meridian per irreducible component, and meridians of intersecting components commute. For irreducible `K` the group is therefore cyclic. Negativity holds for a generic line at infinity and for a general polynomial parametrisation of given degrees; it is exactly the condition our special infinite place can fail. Orevkov’s own degree-(4,6) example that *fails* negativity was computed to have abelian group; that is one example, not a theorem.
103:Neumann–Norbury: for a *nodal* curve the Orevkov invariant equals `π₁(C² − Σ)`, and is abelian when the link at infinity is a positive braid, or for polynomially parametrised curves with sufficiently negative canonical class on each horizontal curve at infinity. Again: nodes, plus a positivity/negativity condition at infinity.
117:**Where the weaker form does die.** If the group is cyclic, it cannot surject onto `S₄`. This happens in the sourced flex case of §2 (`G(d,1) ≅ Z`) and in Orevkov’s negativity+nodes theorem (one generator per component). **Inference:** those two regimes close the weaker form as well as the strong form. They do not cover tacnodes, nor a unibranch singular place at infinity, nor `C^*`.
119:No further desk-scale relation (infinity braid of a Puiseux pair with `q > 1`, product of fibre meridians, etc.) was sourced that would force two disjoint transpositions to coincide or to fail to generate a transitive subgroup. Completing that computation from a hashed local presentation at infinity would be the remaining group-theoretic step; it was not found as a theorem, and it is not carried out here as an unsourced global van Kampen analysis.
127:- Smooth infinite place (flex of order `d−2`) ⇒ Zariski–Oka, `π₁(C² − D)` cyclic. Covers polynomial curves with consecutive degrees, including nodal cubics with the flex at infinity. Does not cover `|deg p − deg q| ≥ 2` or `C^*`.
128:- Affine nodes + Nori 3.27 after resolving infinity to a transverse meeting, with `C̄² > 2 r` ⇒ abelian (Neumann–Norbury recast). Covers ordinary nodes only.
129:- Affine nodes + Orevkov negativity at infinity ⇒ cyclic if irreducible. Negativity is the condition the boxed infinite place can fail.
131:**No sourced countermodel** in the exact class (irreducible, normalisation `A¹` or `C^*`, affine branches smooth). Every standard nonabelian complement uses unibranch singularities, reducibility, or a generic line at infinity with cusps.
133:**Single missing local input.** A Nori-type (or Zariski–van Kampen) statement for an irreducible affine curve whose singularities are double points of *smooth* branches, allowing tangency (`A_{2k−1}`, `k ≥ 2`), on the surface obtained by resolving only the place(s) at infinity — alternatively, a single irreducible polynomial or `C^*` curve of that singularity type with nonabelian `π₁(C² − D)` (or with a transposition-valued `S₄`-surjection as in the weaker form). Until that input is supplied, neither form of the lemma may be promoted. Fail-closed: no gap is filled by the nodal theorems, by the flex theorem, or by analogy with torus-type / cuspidal examples.
